## 10.4.3 - useEffect()

- `useEffect()` is a hook that runs after the component has rendered to the DOM. It takes two arguments:

  ```js
  function ComponentName(props) {
    // ...

    useEffect(() => {
      // Code here will run after the component renders
    }, [])

    return (
      <div>
        {/* ... */}
      </div>
    )
  }
  ```
- The second argument is an array of dependencies. If you pass in `[]`, then it runs only once when the component mounts, and not on every render.

  - If you pass in a dependency like `'foo'`, then it will run after each render where that prop has changed.

  - If you pass in multiple dependencies, then it will run after each render where any of them have changed.
- You can also pass in `null` as the second argument to tell React not to run this effect on every render. This is useful if your effect doesn't depend on props or state, so that it's not recreated for every render.

  ```js
  useEffect(() => {
    // Code here will only run once when the component mounts

    return () => {
      // Clean up code here runs after each render
    }
  }, [])
  ```
- You can also pass in an empty array as the second argument to tell React not to run this effect on every render. This is useful if your effect doesn't depend on props or state, so that it's not recreated for every render.

